definition:
    Italian-American physicist (1901–1954), arguably the last great physicist at home in both theory and experiment. He won the 1938 Nobel Prize for discovering nuclear reactions induced by slow neutrons, then — after fleeing fascist Italy's racial laws — directed the construction of Chicago Pile-1, the world's first self-sustaining chain reaction (December 2, 1942), under Arthur Compton's Met Lab. Fermions, fermium, Fermilab, and the Fermi paradox all carry his name.
